Here are the ready-to-run Azure CLI commands for resource group rg-puppet-training and VM puppet-server, allowing inbound traffic on <YourPort> from source IPs 10.0.0.4 and 10.0.0.5.


//Resolve the NSG Attached to puppet-server
//Run this command to find the Network Security Group name on puppet-server inside rg-puppet-training:


NIC_ID=$(az vm show --resource-group rg-puppet-training --name puppet-server --query "networkProfile.networkInterfaces[0].id" -o tsv)
NSG_NAME=$(az network nic show --ids "$NIC_ID" --query "networkSecurityGroup.id" -o tsv | awk -F'/' '{print $NF}')
echo "NSG Name: $NSG_NAME"

//Verification: Confirm $NSG_NAME outputs a valid NSG name. If it is empty, check the subnet NSG instead.

//Create the Inbound NSG Rule
//Replace <YourPort> with your port number (such as 8140 or 9090), then run:

az network nsg rule create \
  --resource-group rg-puppet-training \
  --nsg-name "$NSG_NAME" \
  --name Allow-Puppet-Sources-Port \
  --priority 1050 \
  --direction Inbound \
  --access Allow \
  --protocol Tcp \
  --source-address-prefixes 10.0.0.4 10.0.0.5 \
  --source-port-ranges '*' \
  --destination-address-prefixes '*' \
  --destination-port-ranges 8140
  
//Verification: Check that the returned JSON shows "provisioningState": "Succeeded", "access": "Allow", and "sourceAddressPrefixes": ["10.0.0.4", "10.0.0.5"].

//Verify the Rule in the NSG
//List the inbound rules to confirm the rule is active:

az network nsg rule list \
  --resource-group rg-puppet-training \
  --nsg-name "$NSG_NAME" \
  --query "[?direction=='Inbound'].{Name:name, Priority:priority, Source:sourceAddressPrefixes, Port:destinationPortRanges, Access:access}" \
  --output table

// Verification: Allow-Puppet-Sources-Port appears with 10.0.0.4 and 10.0.0.5 under Source and your target port under Port.
